1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,565
Pull into a tight parking space,

2
00:00:02,615 --> 00:00:05,400
and your car does something
that looks impossible.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:08,972
On the screen, there it is: your
car, seen from directly above,

4
00:00:09,022 --> 00:00:11,694
with the lines of the
parking bay all around it.

5
00:00:12,494 --> 00:00:13,813
But there's one problem.

6
00:00:13,863 --> 00:00:15,354
There's no camera up there.

7
00:00:15,404 --> 00:00:18,206
No pole, no drone, nothing
floating over the roof.

8
00:00:18,256 --> 00:00:22,428
So how is your car showing you a view
from a place where no camera exists?

9
00:00:23,228 --> 00:00:26,669
The answer involves four
cameras, some clever geometry,

10
00:00:26,719 --> 00:00:31,746
and a computer quietly rebuilding the
world around you, many times every second.

11
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:35,832
Let's start with the hardware.

12
00:00:35,882 --> 00:00:38,379
Most surround-view
systems use four cameras.

13
00:00:38,429 --> 00:00:41,967
One sits at the front, usually
in or near the grille or badge.

14
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:45,439
One is at the rear, often near
the number plate or tailgate.

15
00:00:45,489 --> 00:00:49,432
And one is tucked under each side
mirror, looking down at the ground.

16
00:00:50,232 --> 00:00:52,634
Not every car does it exactly this way.

17
00:00:52,684 --> 00:00:57,287
Some vehicles add extra cameras, and
positions vary between manufacturers.

18
00:00:57,337 --> 00:01:00,934
But four cameras, one on each
side, is the classic layout.

19
00:01:01,734 --> 00:01:04,984
Each of these cameras has an
extremely wide-angle lens,

20
00:01:05,034 --> 00:01:06,664
often called a fisheye lens.

21
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:09,964
Many can take in a field of
view of around 180 degrees.

22
00:01:10,014 --> 00:01:14,164
That's what lets just four cameras
cover a full circle around the car.

23
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:16,033
But there's a catch.

24
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:20,286
To squeeze that much of the world into
one picture, a fisheye lens bends it.

25
00:01:20,336 --> 00:01:21,461
Straight lines curve.

26
00:01:21,511 --> 00:01:23,308
Things at the edges get squashed.

27
00:01:23,358 --> 00:01:25,546
Parking lines look like they're melting.

28
00:01:26,346 --> 00:01:30,468
So the raw footage from these four cameras
looks nothing like a top-down view.

29
00:01:30,518 --> 00:01:34,052
It's four warped images, each looking
out in a different direction.

30
00:01:34,102 --> 00:01:36,352
The trick is turning
them into one picture.

31
00:01:38,152 --> 00:01:40,315
Step one is undoing the distortion.

32
00:01:40,365 --> 00:01:43,603
Engineers measure exactly
how each lens bends light,

33
00:01:43,653 --> 00:01:48,662
and the system uses that model to straighten
the image back out, pixel by pixel.

34
00:01:49,462 --> 00:01:52,390
Step two is the clever part:
changing the point of view.

35
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:55,209
The system assumes the ground
around the car is flat.

36
00:01:55,259 --> 00:01:58,772
If you know exactly where a camera
is, and which way it's pointing,

37
00:01:58,822 --> 00:02:02,761
you can calculate where every point on
that flat ground would appear if you

38
00:02:02,811 --> 00:02:04,516
were looking straight down at it.

39
00:02:04,566 --> 00:02:07,388
Mathematicians call this kind
of mapping a homography.

40
00:02:08,188 --> 00:02:12,906
So each camera's image is reprojected,
as if it had been taken from above.

41
00:02:12,956 --> 00:02:16,579
Front, rear, left and right
become four slices of ground,

42
00:02:16,629 --> 00:02:19,994
each seen from a viewpoint
that doesn't really exist.

43
00:02:20,794 --> 00:02:22,115
Step three is stitching.

44
00:02:22,165 --> 00:02:24,627
Neighbouring cameras
overlap at the corners,

45
00:02:24,677 --> 00:02:28,739
so the system blends those regions
together and evens out differences in

46
00:02:28,789 --> 00:02:31,880
brightness and colour, to
make the seams harder to see.

47
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,050
And the car in the middle?

48
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:36,124
That isn't a photo of your car at all.

49
00:02:36,174 --> 00:02:37,871
The cameras can't see your roof.

50
00:02:37,921 --> 00:02:41,038
It's a graphic, placed into
the empty space in the centre.

51
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:44,150
The whole picture is a composite,
generated in real time.

52
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:47,847
All of this depends on one thing:

53
00:02:47,897 --> 00:02:50,797
the system knowing precisely
where each camera is.

54
00:02:50,847 --> 00:02:54,986
Shift a camera by a small amount, or
tilt it by a fraction of a degree,

55
00:02:55,036 --> 00:02:57,228
and the four slices no longer line up.

56
00:02:58,028 --> 00:03:00,615
That's why surround-view
systems are calibrated.

57
00:03:00,665 --> 00:03:03,363
Typically, patterned targets,
often checkerboards,

58
00:03:03,413 --> 00:03:06,494
are laid on the floor where
neighbouring cameras overlap.

59
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:10,011
Because the system knows exactly
where those patterns should be,

60
00:03:10,061 --> 00:03:12,978
it can work out each camera's
real position and angle.

61
00:03:13,778 --> 00:03:18,188
And if a mirror is replaced, a bumper
is repaired, or a camera gets knocked,

62
00:03:18,238 --> 00:03:20,183
the system may need recalibrating.

63
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:23,410
Skip that, and the view can
show lines that don't meet,

64
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:25,992
or a kerb that seems to
bend at the corners.

65
00:03:27,792 --> 00:03:30,292
Which brings us to what
the system can't do.

66
00:03:30,342 --> 00:03:32,379
Remember the flat ground assumption?

67
00:03:32,429 --> 00:03:34,233
It works well for painted lines.

68
00:03:34,283 --> 00:03:38,638
But anything that sticks up, like a
pole, a bollard, or a person, breaks it.

69
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:41,386
Tall objects get smeared outward,

70
00:03:41,436 --> 00:03:44,473
stretched as if they were
lying flat on the ground.

71
00:03:44,523 --> 00:03:49,135
Near the seams between cameras, an object
can look distorted, appear doubled,

72
00:03:49,185 --> 00:03:50,164
or partly vanish.

73
00:03:50,964 --> 00:03:55,217
There can also be blind spots,
especially very close to the car's body,

74
00:03:55,267 --> 00:03:58,550
and a camera can be hampered
by dirt, rain, or low sun.

75
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:02,914
What you see is a calculated
reconstruction, not a live view from above.

76
00:04:03,714 --> 00:04:07,909
That's why car manufacturers warn
drivers not to rely on the screen alone.

77
00:04:07,959 --> 00:04:11,294
It's a parking aid, not a
replacement for mirrors, sensors,

78
00:04:11,344 --> 00:04:13,072
and actually turning your head.

79
00:04:14,872 --> 00:04:16,449
So let's put it all together.

80
00:04:16,499 --> 00:04:19,646
Four wide-angle cameras capture
the world around the car.

81
00:04:19,696 --> 00:04:21,610
Software corrects their distortion,

82
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:24,358
reprojects each image onto
a flat virtual ground,

83
00:04:24,408 --> 00:04:26,322
and stitches them into one picture,

84
00:04:26,372 --> 00:04:28,958
with an image of your car
placed in the middle.

85
00:04:29,758 --> 00:04:33,004
So next time your car shows you
a bird's-eye view, remember:

86
00:04:33,054 --> 00:04:35,312
there's no camera floating above your car.

87
00:04:35,362 --> 00:04:37,674
Just four cameras, some
clever mathematics,

88
00:04:37,724 --> 00:04:41,300
and a computer doing a ridiculous
amount of work to help you park.

89
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,692
If this changed how you
see that little screen,

90
00:04:44,742 --> 00:04:48,290
subscribe for more of the hidden
science behind everyday things.

91
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:52,787
And tell us in the comments: what everyday
technology should we take apart next?

92
00:04:52,837 --> 00:04:54,698
This has been The Unnecessary Lab.
